cerberus (0.14.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  Session renewal for the cssh client, and the October Go security batch. The
  API and the signer carry no change beyond their dependencies: certificate
  issuance, authorization and the enclave protocol are untouched, and no
  server-side configuration changes. UPGRADE NOTE: cerberus-client is no longer
  Architecture: all, because it now ships a compiled helper.

  * cssh --extend renews the certificate of the session you are already in,
    taking no hostname, account or session id. It needs a certificate carrying
    both empty flag extensions permit-session-renewal@cerberus and
    terminate-on-cert-expiry@cerberus; the remote command delegates to "logsh
    extend", which asks the privileged session supervisor to move the running
    session's deadline.
  * The new cerberus-session helper does the local half. It creates a private
    Unix socket, forwards it through the active SSH connection, and removes its
    local files when ssh exits; authentication, signing and proof of possession
    of the original key all stay on the workstation, so no credential and no
    private key is ever sent to the destination. The bridge pins the original
    certificate's serial, public key, CA and requested principal or mode
    (including a mapped mode such as root-ro), signs a domain-separated nonce
    with the original key, and writes each fresh certificate to a private file.
    Renewal therefore cannot widen a running session: a failed authentication or
    a changed certificate identity leaves the existing deadline in effect.
  * Renewable sessions disable SSH connection multiplexing. Request
    concurrency, agent signing and authentication lifetime are all bounded, and
    signing subprocesses are terminated when a renewal is cancelled.
  * The destination's sshd needs AcceptEnv CERBERUS_RENEW_SOCKET,
    AllowStreamLocalForwarding remote, and AllowTcpForwarding remote with
    PermitListen none in the applicable Match block. OpenSSH 10.5 gates remote
    Unix-socket forwarding on the TCP forwarding permission, so
    AllowTcpForwarding no blocks the bridge, while PermitListen none keeps
    remote TCP listeners refused. See docs/cssh.md for the full recipe.
  * UPGRADE NOTE: cerberus-client now builds for amd64 and arm64 rather than
    Architecture: all, because it installs /usr/bin/cerberus-session beside the
    cssh profile script. A mirror or deployment pipeline that assumed one
    architecture-independent client package has to fetch the per-architecture
    build instead. The RPM and Arch packagings made the same change.
  * The Go toolchain is pinned to 1.27.2 and golang.org/x/net raised to v0.60.0
    in all three modules, which clears the October advisory batch (GO-2026-6603,
    6605, 6607, 6608, 6610, 6611, 6613 and 6617): eight vulnerabilities
    govulncheck reported as called, in net/http, net/http/internal/http2,
    net/textproto and crypto/tls.
  * CI moves golangci-lint to v2.14.0 and builds gosec against
    golang.org/x/tools v0.50.0. Go 1.27.2 emits export-data version 5, which no
    released gosec and no earlier golangci-lint can read, so both gates failed
    on the toolchain bump with import errors rather than findings.
  * SECURITY.md states how to report a vulnerability -- GitHub private
    reporting or the maintainer's address, never a public issue -- and that only
    the latest release receives fixes.
  * .oss-scanner/ adds the build environment and threat-model summary used by
    Anthropic's OSS Scanner, which builds the image with the network on and
    then analyses it with the network off.
  * AWS SDK v2 refresh in both service modules: core 1.47.0 -> 1.47.1, kms
    1.61.0 -> 1.61.2 (the attested Decrypt the host performs on the enclave's
    behalf), config 1.33.5 -> 1.33.7, credentials 1.20.5 -> 1.20.7, sts 1.51.0
    -> 1.51.2, imds 1.20.0 -> 1.20.1, plus the signin, sso, ssooidc, v4a,
    presigned-url, configsources and endpoints set. ssh-cert-api additionally
    moves go-ldap/ldap/v3 to 3.4.15 and golang.org/x/sync to 0.24.0.
  * CI gains tests/cssh_extend_test.sh, run under bash, zsh and sh beside the
    existing sidecar and login-as suites.
  * govulncheck reports no vulnerabilities reachable from any module.

 -- Paul Kilar <pkilar@gmail.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:58:12 -0400

cerberus (0.13.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  Identity-scoped access, and a fix for a setting that silently did nothing.
  Both new features are opt-in and an existing configuration behaves exactly as
  before, with one exception: a service_principal set to a bare hostname now
  refuses to start, because that value could never have matched a keytab entry.

  * allowed_principals mapping targets may now be the reserved "$self", which
    issues the requesting user's own short uid instead of a fixed name.
    "- root: $self" gives each member of a group a certificate naming them
    individually, so sshd's AuthorizedPrincipalsFile can map humans onto a
    shared account and every login is attributable in the host's own auth log.
  * $self resolves through the same gate as self_principal (enabled, the realms
    allowlist and the deny list), so no uid becomes issuable that
    "self_principal: true" could not already obtain, and a group using it while
    that block is disabled is refused at config load. A caller the gate refuses
    is a coverage miss, so the next group alphabetically still gets a turn,
    logged as authz.self_target.ineligible at Debug; it is never a silent grant.
  * certificate_rules.login_as_issued marks a group's certificates with the
    reserved flag extension login-as-principal@cerberus. sshd ignores it; cssh
    reads it as "log in as this certificate's single principal, not the name
    typed", which lets a requested name act as a mode selector: cssh
    root-ro@host against a "root-ro: $self" mapping connects you to your own
    account. It is applied only to single-principal certificates, announced on
    stderr when the login name changes, and skipped for --sign-only. The
    extension name is reserved; config load refuses a group that sets it
    through static_attributes.
  * /sign now refuses a requested principal beginning with "$". Config already
    reserved the prefix on the requestable side of a mapping, but a group
    granting "*" matched any requested name and would otherwise have issued a
    certificate for a principal literally called "$self".
  * service_principal now selects the keytab entry that decrypts incoming
    service tickets. It was passed to gokrb5's client-side SName option, which
    server-side verification never reads, so the setting had no effect and a
    client reaching the API under a name other than the keytab's SPN failed
    with "matching key not found in keytab". UPGRADE NOTE: a bare hostname is
    now refused at config load, since it can never match a two-component HTTP/
    keytab entry. Give it a full service principal such as
    "HTTP/cerberus.example.com", optionally suffixed with a realm.
  * Rollout order for the new features: upgrade cerberus-client before enabling
    a mapping, because an older cssh re-signs on every call once the server
    rewrites a requested name, and move every API instance to the new
    configuration together so clients see one policy fingerprint.
  * AWS SDK v2 refresh in both service modules: core 1.46.0 -> 1.47.0, kms
    1.59.0 -> 1.61.0 (the attested Decrypt the host performs on the enclave's
    behalf), sts 1.49.0 -> 1.51.0, imds 1.19.2 -> 1.20.0, config to 1.33.5 and
    credentials to 1.20.5, plus the signin, sso, ssooidc, v4a, presigned-url,
    configsources and endpoints set.
  * CI gains tests/cssh_login_as_test.sh, run under both bash and zsh beside
    the existing sidecar suite.
  * govulncheck reports no vulnerabilities reachable from any module.

 -- Paul Kilar <pkilar@gmail.com>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 21:40:45 -0400

cerberus (0.12.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  Dependency-only release. No behavioural change to the API, the signer,
  vsock-watch, or the cssh client, and no configuration change: upgrading from
  0.12.0 needs nothing beyond installing the new packages.

  * OIDC dependency refresh on the bearer-token authentication path:
    coreos/go-oidc/v3 to 3.21.0 and go-jose/v4 to 4.1.5, the latter being the
    JWS verification used to validate access tokens.
  * golang.org/x/crypto 0.55.0 -> 0.57.0 across all three modules, plus x/net
    to 0.59.0, x/sync to 0.23.0, x/sys to 0.48.0, x/oauth2 to 0.37.0 and
    x/time to 0.16.0.
  * AWS SDK v2 refresh in both service modules: core to 1.46.0, config to
    1.33.3, kms to 1.59.0 (the attested Decrypt path), and the accompanying
    credentials, imds, endpoints, v4a, presigned-url, signin, sso, ssooidc and
    sts indirect set.
  * mdlayher/socket to 0.7.0, under the VSOCK transport both services use.
  * The sweep that closes this release moves all three modules together rather
    than one at a time. The submodules replace github.com/pkilar/cerberus with
    ../, so MVS folds the root's requirements into their builds and a shared
    dependency raised in one module alone breaks the other two. It also brings
    each submodule's stale github.com/pkilar/cerberus pin from v0.10.6 up to
    v0.12.0; the replace directive means that version never affected a build,
    but it had drifted three releases behind what the code is written against.
  * govulncheck reports no vulnerabilities in any module. Nothing in this
    release is advisory-driven.

